  "original_commits": [
    {
      "sha": "0d997632754888debc0f7b25fec2dbc59104ca74",
      "parent": "df3ff68984d8048c2433c751b35aec06d90bdc8a",
      "author": {
        "name": "Timothy Schmidt",
        "email": "timschmidt@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-10-31T09:30:04Z"
      },
      "message": "dt-bindings: media: Add the MSM8226 CAMSS\n\nMSM8226 has the CSIPHY, CSID, ISPIF and VFE blocks of MSM8916 at the same\noffsets and interrupts, without the separate CAMSS AHB clock.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
    },
    {
      "sha": "f8a7ccc43396a809eb935100aa6db70a7defa3a3",
      "parent": "0d997632754888debc0f7b25fec2dbc59104ca74",
      "author": {
        "name": "Timothy Schmidt",
        "email": "timschmidt@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-10-31T09:30:05Z"
      },
      "message": "media: qcom: camss: Add MSM8226 resources\n\nDescribe MSM8226 with the MSM8916 operations: two 2-phase CSIPHYs, two CSID\n4.1 instances, one ISPIF and one VFE. Drop the separate CAMSS AHB clock,\nwhich MSM8226 lacks, and use the MSM8226 VFE clock rates (37.5 to 400 MHz).\n\nThe vendor kernel treats the MSM8226 VFE as version 4.0 with its own VBIF\nsetup; reusing the 4.1 operations is still to be verified on hardware.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
    },
    {
      "sha": "8203cfa85fcefda97defbc1e9a98317eedf1d2b9",
      "parent": "f8a7ccc43396a809eb935100aa6db70a7defa3a3",
      "author": {
        "name": "Timothy Schmidt",
        "email": "timschmidt@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-10-31T09:30:05Z"
      },
      "message": "ARM: dts: qcom: msm8226: Describe the CAMSS\n\nAdd the camera subsystem with its clocks, interrupts, VFE GDSC and the VFE\nIOMMU. It stays disabled until a board connects sensors or uses the CSID\ntest pattern generator.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"
    }
  ],
  "AXI_followup": "d1f8047db3854e5b6ba309b3ac0e130224b11136",
